Q: Is there a way to intercept `beacon` requests and `service-worker.js` downloads with puppeteer? A: <p>The issue is that <code>page.setRequestInterception()</code> only catches requests initiated by the page context. Service worker script fetches and <code>navigator.sendBeacon()</code> calls go through a different network path that Puppeteer's high-level interception doesn't cover.</p>
<p>Here's how to handle both:</p>
<p><strong>For service-worker.js downloads</strong>, use the CDP <code>Fetch</code> domain instead of <code>page.setRequestInterception()</code>. It intercepts at the network layer, before Chrome decides which context handles the request:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const client = await page.target().createCDPSession();

await client.send('Fetch.enable', {
  patterns: [{ urlPattern: '*', requestStage: 'Request' }],
});

client.on('Fetch.requestPaused', async (event) =&gt; {
  const headers = event.request.headers;

  // Add your custom headers
  headers['X-My-Header'] = 'value';

  await client.send('Fetch.continueRequest', {
    requestId: event.requestId,
    headers: Object.entries(headers).map(([name, value]) =&gt; ({ name, value })),
  });
});
</code></pre>
<p>This catches everything including service worker script downloads, because <code>Fetch.enable</code> operates at the browser network stack level rather than the page level.</p>
<p><strong>For beacon requests</strong>, there's a catch — <code>navigator.sendBeacon()</code> fires with <code>keepalive: true</code>, and Chrome may send these after the page context is gone (e.g. during <code>unload</code>). The CDP <code>Fetch</code> domain above will intercept beacons that fire while the page is still alive. But beacons sent during page teardown may slip through because Chrome intentionally sends them without waiting for interception.</p>
<p>If you absolutely need to catch every beacon, you can override <code>navigator.sendBeacon</code> before the page loads:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>await page.evaluateOnNewDocument(() =&gt; {
  const originalBeacon = navigator.sendBeacon.bind(navigator);
  navigator.sendBeacon = function(url, data) {
    // Convert to a regular fetch so interception can catch it
    fetch(url, {
      method: 'POST',
      body: data,
      headers: { 'X-My-Header': 'value' },
      keepalive: true,
    });
    return true;
  };
});
</code></pre>
<p>This replaces <code>sendBeacon</code> with a <code>fetch</code> call that goes through normal interception while preserving <code>keepalive</code> behavior.</p>
<p><strong>TL;DR</strong>: Replace <code>page.setRequestInterception()</code> with CDP <code>Fetch.enable</code> for service worker scripts. For beacons, either use the same CDP approach (covers most cases) or override <code>navigator.sendBeacon</code> via <code>evaluateOnNewDocument</code> for full coverage.</p>

Q: What is the default CSS stylesheet PhantomJS uses when rendering PDF/HTML, and how to replicate it in Puppeteer? A: <p>I went through a similar PhantomJS → Puppeteer migration. The visual differences come from three layers, not just the UA stylesheet.</p>
<p><strong>1. The UA stylesheet difference</strong></p>
<p>PhantomJS 2.x used a QtWebKit fork frozen around WebKit r187AE (late 2015). The exact stylesheet is bundled in the Qt source:</p>
<pre><code>qt5/qtwebkit/Source/WebCore/css/html.css
</code></pre>
<p>You can find it in the <a href="https://github.com/nicola/phantomjs2/blob/master/test/standards/thirdparty/html.css" rel="nofollow noreferrer">archived QtWebKit repo</a>. But honestly, the UA stylesheet differences are minor — mostly margin/padding on <code>body</code>, <code>h1</code>–<code>h6</code>, lists, and tables.</p>
<p><strong>2. The real culprits</strong></p>
<p>What actually causes visible differences:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong><code>printBackground: true</code></strong> — you already have this, good. PhantomJS printed backgrounds by default, Puppeteer doesn't.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Font rendering</strong> — QtWebKit used the system's font stack differently. Puppeteer/Chromium renders fonts through Skia. Add explicit fonts in your CSS:</p>
</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>body {
  font-family: 'Helvetica Neue', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
  -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
}
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><strong>Default print margins</strong> — PhantomJS with <code>margin: '0cm'</code> truly zeroed everything. Puppeteer's <code>@page</code> rule can still add spacing. Force it:</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>@page { margin: 0; }
* { box-sizing: border-box; }
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><strong>Table rendering</strong> — QtWebKit had different default <code>border-spacing</code> and <code>border-collapse</code>. Add:</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>table { border-collapse: collapse; border-spacing: 0; }
</code></pre>
<p><strong>3. Compatibility CSS snippet</strong></p>
<p>Inject this before generating the PDF to get closer to PhantomJS output:</p>
<pre><code>await page.addStyleTag({ content: `
  @page { margin: 0; size: A4; }
  html, body { margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  body {
    font-family: 'Helvetica Neue', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.2;
    -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact;
  }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; border-spacing: 0; }
  img { max-width: 100%; }
`});
</code></pre>
<p>And make sure you set <code>preferCSSPageSize: true</code> in the pdf options:</p>
<pre><code>await page.pdf({
  path: pdfTempFilePath,
  preferCSSPageSize: true,
  printBackground: true,
  margin: { top: '0cm', bottom: '0cm', left: '0cm', right: '0cm' }
});
</code></pre>
<p>The real fix is iterative — compare specific elements side by side and override the CSS differences as you find them. There's no single &quot;make it look like PhantomJS&quot; switch, but the snippet above covers 90% of cases.</p>
